  "description": "<p><b>The task here is to clarify the best practice in the documentation.</b></p>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p>Because of the way that acl:default takes the ACL and re-writes it onto child objects, then any object that is underneath gets the same permissions.</p>\n\n<p>Because you would need the parent object to have the class defined in the acl:accessToClass, then why not specify acl:accessTo.</p>\n\n<p>If instead you use acl:default and acl:accessToClass, then this seems to be a method to assign these permissions to a subset of the children (those with the specified class).</p>\n\n<p>ie.</p>\n\n<p>/rest/trunk</p>\n\n<p>&lt;&gt; a pcdm-use:Image</p>\n\n<p>/rest/trunk/fcr:acl</p>\n\n<p> &lt;&gt; a webac:Acl .<br/>\n  &lt;#auth1&gt; a acl:Authorization ;<br/>\n     acl:accessToClass pcdm-use:Image ;<br/>\n     acl:mode acl:Read ;<br/>\n     acl:agent \"somebody\" ;<br/>\n     acl:default &lt;/rest/trunk&gt; .</p>\n\n<p>Then any child item that shares the type pcdm-use:Image would get these authorizations, others would continue up the tree.</p>\n\n<p> </p>",
